wn, beside the version and the rule it was made under: &#x60;eu&#x60; counts only a choice made under &#x60;eu&#x60;, and the policy floor voids a choice&#39;s grants, never its refusals. Each event states the grants in force when it is sent, and that is what cloud may forward under.  &#x60;data-banner&#x60; draws Hanzo&#39;s consent design by region. In &#x60;eu&#x60;, for a visitor who has not chosen and whose browser has not refused: a card with Manage preferences, Accept all and a × that keeps essential cookies only, so refusing is as easy as accepting. In &#x60;global&#x60;, a notice (\&quot;We use cookies to improve your experience. By continuing to browse, you agree.\&quot;) until the visitor closes it with ×. In &#x60;us_privacy&#x60;, no card: a \&quot;Your Privacy Choices\&quot; link with the opt-out icon at the foot of the page, unless the page carries its own (an element marked &#x60;data-hz-consent-link&#x60;). Manage preferences or the link opens the sheet — Strictly Necessary Cookies (always active, the site&#39;s own first-party analytics included), Performance (Google Analytics), Marketing (the Meta pixel), and Sell, Share, or Use Your Data for Targeted Advertising, each chosen apart; outside &#x60;eu&#x60; each is on by default and Targeted Advertising off is the opt-out. Withdrawing a category clears the third-party cookies it allowed, on every domain above the page, and never an essential one. The sheet links only the policies the page names: &#x60;data-cookies&#x60; its cookie policy, &#x60;data-banner&#x60;&#39;s value its privacy policy, &#x60;data-terms&#x60; its terms. &#x60;window.hzConsent.open()&#x60; opens the sheet again from a page&#39;s own link.  WITHOUT A KEY THE TAG SENDS NOTHING. A keyless beacon is accepted 200 into $public, a reserved tenant the owning org cannot read — so silence is the honest failure, and the tag picks it rather than reporting success into a tenant nobody reads.
