        /// <summary>
        /// The Hanzo event tag — the one-line install for a surface with no bundler Serves the browser tag that autocaptures pageviews (initial and SPA) and uncaught errors onto the canonical wire at POST /v1/event.  Install is one line, and it is the same line for a Hanzo property and for a customer&#39;s own page:      &lt;script defer src&#x3D;\&quot;https://api.hanzo.ai/v1/event/tag.js\&quot; data-key&#x3D;\&quot;pk-…\&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;  &#x60;data-key&#x60; is the publishable key the project mints; &#x60;data-product&#x60; optionally names the emitting surface. The key may also ride the src as &#x60;?key&#x3D;&#x60; for a host that strips data attributes.  THE STREAM IS FIRST PARTY; THE THIRD PARTIES FOLLOW THE VISITOR&#39;S REGION. Every page view, event, error and identify goes to the site&#39;s own stream for every visitor, under its anonymous id and session, whatever they chose and under Global Privacy Control or Do Not Track alike: it is the site&#39;s own analytics, never sold or shared. The rule for Google Analytics, the Meta pixel and the other platforms is the one GET /v1/project/tags serves for the visitor&#39;s region. &#x60;eu&#x60; (the EU, the EEA, the UK, Switzerland, and any visitor no edge placed): Google Analytics runs for everyone under Consent Mode v2 with all four flags denied (cookieless) and is upgraded by Performance and Targeted Advertising; Meta is init&#39;ed after &#x60;fbq(&#39;consent&#39;,&#39;revoke&#39;)&#x60; and sends nothing until Marketing; no other platform loads before a choice. &#x60;us_privacy&#x60; (a US state with a privacy law, or no attested state) and &#x60;global&#x60;: everything runs at once under Consent Mode granted, and Targeted Advertising off is exactly &#x60;fbq(&#39;dataProcessingOptions&#39;,[&#39;LDU&#39;],0,0)&#x60; and ad_storage, ad_user_data and ad_personalization denied. Under GPC or DNT no third party loads in any region, Google Analytics included. The choice is the &#x60;__Host-hz_consent&#x60; cookie, written by the @hanzo/ui consent banner or this tag&#39;s own, beside the version and the rule it was made under: &#x60;eu&#x60; counts only a choice made under &#x60;eu&#x60;, and the policy floor voids a choice&#39;s grants, never its refusals. Each event states the grants in force when it is sent, and that is what cloud may forward under.  &#x60;data-banner&#x60; draws Hanzo&#39;s consent design by region. In &#x60;eu&#x60;, for a visitor who has not chosen and whose browser has not refused: a card with Manage preferences, Accept all and a × that keeps essential cookies only, so refusing is as easy as accepting. In &#x60;global&#x60;, a notice (\&quot;We use cookies to improve your experience. By continuing to browse, you agree.\&quot;) until the visitor closes it with ×. In &#x60;us_privacy&#x60;, no card: a \&quot;Your Privacy Choices\&quot; link with the opt-out icon at the foot of the page, unless the page carries its own (an element marked &#x60;data-hz-consent-link&#x60;). Manage preferences or the link opens the sheet — Strictly Necessary Cookies (always active, the site&#39;s own first-party analytics included), Performance (Google Analytics), Marketing (the Meta pixel), and Sell, Share, or Use Your Data for Targeted Advertising, each chosen apart; outside &#x60;eu&#x60; each is on by default and Targeted Advertising off is the opt-out. Withdrawing a category clears the third-party cookies it allowed, on every domain above the page, and never an essential one. The sheet links only the policies the page names: &#x60;data-cookies&#x60; its cookie policy, &#x60;data-banner&#x60;&#39;s value its privacy policy, &#x60;data-terms&#x60; its terms. &#x60;window.hzConsent.open()&#x60; opens the sheet again from a page&#39;s own link.  WITHOUT A KEY THE TAG SENDS NOTHING. A keyless beacon is accepted 200 into $public, a reserved tenant the owning org cannot read — so silence is the honest failure, and the tag picks it rather than reporting success into a tenant nobody reads.
